  { id:'opmcq-c7-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"A 34-year-old woman presents with a 2-day history of eye redness and mild discomfort in her left eye. Vision is normal, and there is no photophobia. Slit-lamp examination shows sectoral redness with engorged superficial episcleral vessels that move slightly with a cotton-tipped applicator. The ophthalmologist instills 2.5% phenylephrine, and the redness blanches partially within minutes, while deeper vessels remain unaffected. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Anterior scleritis","Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis","Episcleritis","Acute allergic conjunctivitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The stem is built out of four findings, and three of them are printed signs of episcleritis.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98 gives the disease in five lines and the stem quotes them almost in order:\n\n> – Relatively common, benign, **self-limited** cause of red eye\n> – **(2/3)** of cases are **unilateral**\n> – More common in **females** than in males **(3:1)**\n>\n> Signs: **Sectoral or diffuse injection** · Chemosis · Tender episcleral nodules that can be **easily mobilized over the sclera**\n\nA 34-year-old woman, one eye, two days, **sectoral** redness, and vessels that **move** over the sclera under a cotton bud. Mobility is the finding that does the most work here and it is the one the bank rarely gives: episcleral vessels sit in a loose vascular layer above the sclera and slide over it, while the deep episcleral plexus that inflames in scleritis is anchored and does not.\n\n| Option | What the course requires | Present? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Anterior scleritis** | **Violet-bluish** hue, scleral oedema, **moderate to severe pain** with globe tenderness, **no** blanching at phenylephrine 10 % (p.99–100) | No. Vision is normal, the discomfort is mild, and the vessels move — scleritis vessels do not |\n| **B. Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis** | Watery discharge, **follicles**, **preauricular lymphadenopathy**, usually bilateral (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.90) | Nothing. No discharge, no node, no follicle, and the redness is sectoral rather than diffuse |\n| **C. Episcleritis** | Sectoral injection, mobile superficial vessels, unilateral, female, normal vision | The key |\n| **D. Acute allergic conjunctivitis** | **Itching**, chemosis, mild papillary reaction, bilateral (p.87) | Itching is never mentioned, and allergic disease is not sectoral |\n\n⚠️ **THE STEM CONTRADICTS THE COURSE ON ONE POINT, AND IT IS RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED.** The bank has 2.5 % phenylephrine blanching this eye partially. Both course sources print the opposite. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.101 and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 40 print the same 3×2 table:\n\n| | Phenylephrine 2.5 % | Phenylephrine 10 % |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Conjunctivitis** | blanches | blanches |\n| **Episcleritis** | **does NOT blanch** | blanches |\n| **Anterior scleritis** | does not blanch | does not blanch |\n\nOn that table, 2.5 % is the drop that separates **conjunctivitis** from episcleritis, not the drop that confirms episcleritis. The printed key is nevertheless the only tenable option, because the stem hands over the diagnosis twice more — mobile superficial episcleral vessels, and deep vessels unaffected — and because *partial* blanching at a low concentration is not the same claim as the table's clean cross. **The key is not disputed; the divergence is noted.** For the version of this test that the course does endorse, see `opmcq-c7-4` and `opmcq-c7-5`, which are the table asked directly.\n\n⚠️ **Photophobia is a trap in the other direction.** The stem says there is none, which reads as evidence against inflammation — but `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98 lists \"Discomfort photophobia or tenderness\" among episcleritis symptoms, so its absence does not exclude the key either. It excludes keratitis and uveitis, neither of which is offered.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain topic 7 works the same disease from the opposite end — `opqb-t7-296` gives a 27-year-old woman with sectoral redness and a **mobile episcleral nodule** and asks for the commonest **cause** (idiopathic) rather than the diagnosis. Same signs, different question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Sectoral unilateral redness with superficial episcleral vessels that move over the sclera, in a young woman with normal vision and mild discomfort, is episcleritis — and the course's own phenylephrine table makes 10 %, not 2.5 %, the drop that blanches it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.49 (Ch.7 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"A 50-year-old man presents to the GP with a complaint of severe eye pain, redness and decreased vision. Examination was carried out and it revealed that the redness had violet-bluish hue with scleral edema. Phenylephrine 10% was used topically for a few minutes but the redness didn't disappear. Which of the following would be the most appropriate next step in order to properly treat that patient?",
    options:["Conservative management as it is a self-limited condition","Massive doses of steroids","Urgent referral to an ophthalmologist","Broad spectrum antibiotics"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Every clinical word here is a printed sign of scleritis, and the treatment line is printed too.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100:\n\n> Signs: The redness has a characteristic **violet-bluish** hue with **scleral oedema** and overlying chemosis.\n> **No blanching** occurs with **phenylephrine 10%** (D.D. from episcleritis)\n>\n> Treatment: • Scleritis is a **vision-threatening** condition that requires **urgent referral to an ophthalmologist.**\n\nThat last sentence is the answer, verbatim. The whole stem exists to establish scleritis rather than episcleritis, and the failure to blanch at **10 %** — the high concentration — is what does it.\n\n**The question is asked of a GP, and that framing is the point.** `L37) Red Eye.txt`, which is a book chapter rather than a slide deck, is written for exactly this reader: its stated objective is to \"determine whether a patient with a red eye requires the prompt attention of an ophthalmologist or whether you can appropriately evaluate and treat the condition\". The correct primary-care action for scleritis is to hand it on, not to start treating it.\n\n| Option | The course's position | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Conservative, it is self-limited** | ⚠️ \"Self-limited\" belongs to **episcleritis**, not scleritis — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99 and p.102 both use the phrase, and both use it in the episcleritis column only. The whole stem was constructed to exclude episcleritis | The designed trap: right sentence, wrong disease |\n| **B. Massive doses of steroids** | Systemic corticosteroids **are** part of scleritis treatment (p.100) — but after referral, at a specified dose, alongside NSAIDs and immunomodulators, and monitored. \"Massive doses\" started by a GP is neither the printed regimen nor a safe one | Partly true, wrongly placed |\n| **C. Urgent referral** | p.100, verbatim | The key |\n| **D. Broad spectrum antibiotics** | Scleritis is not primarily infective. Its printed causes are RA first, other collagen vascular disease, gout, **infections (TB, Syphilis, Herpes zoster)**, injury, idiopathic — infection is one item of six and none of those three is treated with a broad-spectrum agent | No |\n\n**B is the option worth arguing with.** Steroids are genuinely in the regimen, and a reader who has memorised the drug list without the sequence will take it. Two things defeat it: the question asks for the **next step**, and the course's next step is a referral; and the printed regimen is a *ladder* — oral NSAIDs, then systemic corticosteroids, then immunomodulating drugs — not a single massive dose. ⚠️ Note also that `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 39 lists the treatment as \"Oral NSAIDs · Systemic corticosteroids · Immunomodulating drugs · Surgical intervention\" and **never mentions referral at all**. The referral sentence is the book's alone, which is why the book is the citation on this key.\n\n⚠️ **This stem is the antecedent of `opmcq-c7-3`.** Read them together.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t7-295` asks the treatment question for the severe end of the same disease — a 61-year-old with RA, scleral thinning and exposed uvea — and keys \"Systemic steroids + immunosuppressants\", i.e. what happens *after* the referral this question asks for.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A violet-bluish red eye with scleral oedema and severe pain that does not blanch with phenylephrine 10 % is scleritis, and the book's printed first step is urgent referral because it is vision-threatening — self-limited belongs to episcleritis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.49 (Ch.7 Q2; the stem is printed with a line break mid-sentence after "would be the most")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"A 50-year-old man has severe eye pain, redness and decreased vision. The redness has a violet-bluish hue with scleral edema, and topical phenylephrine 10% did not make it disappear. What is the most feared complication that might occur if this patient is NOT properly managed?",
    options:["Spread of infection","Chronicity","Recurrence","Vision loss","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most feared complication that might occur if the patient is NOT properly managed?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c7-2`, printed immediately above it on the same book page, and it was read rather than assumed. The stem above restates that patient's **findings** and nothing else. Two things are deliberately withheld: the antecedent's own key (urgent referral) — naming it would answer `opmcq-c7-2` — and the word *vision-threatening*, which is the book's phrase and would hand over this question's answer in its own stem.\n\n**The answer is the adjective the book uses for the disease.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100:\n\n> Scleritis is a **vision-threatening** condition that requires urgent referral to an ophthalmologist.\n\nand its prognosis paragraph on the same page:\n\n> - **Visual loss** is related to the **severity** of the scleritis.\n> - Patients with **necrotizing scleritis** have a **high incidence of visual loss** and an **increased mortality rate**.\n\nNothing else on the page is called feared, and nothing else is called threatening.\n\n| Option | Where it belongs | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Spread of infection** | Scleritis is usually not infective at all — infection is one of six printed cause categories (TB, syphilis, herpes zoster) and is never described as spreading | Wrong mechanism for the disease |\n| **B. Chronicity** | Real and printed: \"Scleritis may be active for **several months or years** before going into long-term remission\" (p.100) | ⚠️ True but not the *most feared*. A protracted course is unpleasant; a blind eye is the outcome the referral exists to prevent |\n| **C. Recurrence** | Recurrence is the word the course attaches to **episcleritis** (\"Patients who experience **recurrent** attacks may require systemic evaluation\", p.99), not to scleritis | Right word, wrong disease — the same misdirection `opmcq-c7-2` used with \"self-limited\" |\n| **D. Vision loss** | p.100, twice | The key |\n| **E. None of the above** | — | Fails because D is printed |\n\n**B is the only option that survives a first reading**, and the discrimination is the word *feared* rather than *likely*. The book supports both statements; it ranks only one. ⚠️ And there is a fact behind the fact that makes the ranking sharper than the options show: p.100 gives necrotizing scleritis an **increased mortality rate**, so the most feared outcome of untreated scleritis in the round is arguably not ocular at all. The bank does not offer death as an option, and the printed key is the worst ocular outcome on the menu.\n\n⚠️ **The mortality association is the book's alone.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slides 34–39 cover anterior, necrotizing and posterior scleritis, causes and treatment, and print **no prognosis line of any kind** — no visual-loss statement, no mortality. A reader working only from the deck could not rank these five options.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Scleritis is the book's vision-threatening red eye: visual loss tracks severity, necrotizing disease carries both high visual loss and increased mortality, and chronicity and recurrence — the latter an episcleritis word — are lesser outcomes.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.49 (Ch.7 Q3; the stem is printed "In the previous case, what is the most feared complication that might occur if the patient is NOT properly managed?" and refers back to Ch.7 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"How can you differentiate between episcleritis and scleritis?",
    options:["Using phenylephrine 2.5%, redness disappears in episcleritis","Using phenylephrine 2.5%, redness disappears in scleritis","Using phenylephrine 10%, redness disappears in episcleritis","Using phenylephrine 10%, redness disappears in scleritis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**One row of one table, asked directly.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.101 is a page headed \"NOTES:\" whose entire purpose is this test, and it prints the rule as a sentence before printing it as a grid:\n\n> **Differentiation** between episcleritis and Scleritis can be done by instilling a drop of phenylephrine **10%** in the affected eye. Blanching is seen in **episcleritis** with the disappearance of hyperaemia while **not in scleritis**.\n\n| | Phenylephrine 2.5 % | Phenylephrine 10 % |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Conjunctivitis** | blanches | blanches |\n| **Episcleritis** | does not blanch | **blanches** |\n| **Anterior scleritis** | does not blanch | **does not blanch** |\n\nThe two variables are concentration and disease, and the four options are the 2×2 of getting either one wrong. **10 % + episcleritis blanches** is the only cell that separates the pair, so **C**.\n\n**Why the concentrations differ is the mechanism worth holding.** Phenylephrine is a vasoconstrictor, and how deep it reaches depends on how much of it there is. At 2.5 % it constricts the superficial conjunctival vessels only; at 10 % it penetrates far enough to constrict the episcleral plexus as well. Nothing at either strength reaches the deep scleral vessels, which is why scleritis is the row of two crosses. *(The depth-of-penetration explanation is **not taken from the course material** — both sources print the results and neither prints a mechanism.)*\n\n| Option | Fault |\n|---|---|\n| **A.** 2.5 % / episcleritis | Wrong concentration. 2.5 % is the conjunctivitis drop — this is the exact error `opmcq-c7-1`'s stem makes |\n| **B.** 2.5 % / scleritis | Both wrong. Scleritis blanches at neither strength |\n| **C.** 10 % / episcleritis | The key |\n| **D.** 10 % / scleritis | Right concentration, inverted result. Blanching at 10 % is what *excludes* scleritis |\n\n⚠️ **Both course sources print this table and they agree exactly** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.101 and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 40, the deck's closing slide, whose title is \"Blanching effect of Phenylephrine eye drops to differentiate conjunctivitis, episcleritis and anterior scleritis\". ⚠️ The book contradicts *itself* elsewhere, though, and the contradiction is recorded in this project's chapter cache: p.98 says the 2.5 % test shows blanching \"in conjunctivitis … while not in episcleritis\", while p.102's table says of episcleritis \"**Blanching** occurs with **phenylephrine 10 %**\". The p.101 grid reconciles them, and this question is keyed on the grid.\n\n**Its twin is `opmcq-c7-5`**, which substitutes conjunctivitis for scleritis and keeps the same four-option scaffold; `opmcq-c7-17` asks the same fact a third time as one limb of a Both-A-&-B question, and `opmcq-c7-22` a fourth time as a distractor. **Four of this chapter's twenty-six questions turn on this one table.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Phenylephrine 10 % blanches episcleritis and does not blanch scleritis; 2.5 % blanches only conjunctivitis. Scleritis blanches at neither concentration.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.50 (Ch.7 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"How can you differentiate between episcleritis and conjunctivitis?",
    options:["Using phenylephrine 2.5%, redness disappears in episcleritis","Using phenylephrine 2.5%, redness disappears in conjunctivitis","Using phenylephrine 10%, redness disappears in episcleritis","Using phenylephrine 10%, redness disappears in conjunctivitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The other row of the table in `opmcq-c7-4`, and the full grid is written out there.** `ophthalmology.pdf` prints this one twice — once inside the episcleritis signs on p.98 and again on the p.101 NOTES page:\n\n> **Differentiation** between episcleritis and conjunctivitis can be done by instilling a drop of phenylephrine **2.5%** in the affected eye. Blanching of the conjunctival is seen in **conjunctivitis** with the disappearance of hyperaemia while **not in episcleritis**.\n\nSo **B**.\n\n⚠️ **OPTION D IS ALSO A TRUE STATEMENT, AND IT IS NOT A DEFECT.** Conjunctivitis does blanch at 10 % — the table gives it a tick in both columns. What D is not is a *differentiator*: at 10 % episcleritis blanches too, so a blanched eye at that concentration tells you nothing about which of the two you are looking at. The stem's verb is \"differentiate\", and only the 2.5 % column has a disagreement in it. This is the same shape as an \"all are true except\" question where two options are true and one is truer; here two options are factually correct and only one answers the question asked.\n\n| Option | True? | Differentiates? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A.** 2.5 % / episcleritis | **False** — episcleritis does not blanch at 2.5 % | — |\n| **B.** 2.5 % / conjunctivitis | True | **Yes** — the only cell where the two diseases differ. The key |\n| **C.** 10 % / episcleritis | True (and it is `opmcq-c7-4`'s key) | No — conjunctivitis blanches at 10 % as well |\n| **D.** 10 % / conjunctivitis | True | ⚠️ No — for the same reason as C, from the other side |\n\n**The clinical reading is simpler than the grid.** Conjunctival vessels are the most superficial of the three layers, so the weakest vasoconstrictor reaches them; episcleral vessels need the strong drop; scleral vessels are reached by neither. Read down the table and it is a depth ladder. *(The layer-depth reading is **not taken from the course material**; both sources print results without mechanism.)*\n\n⚠️ **Practical caution the course does not print:** this test is described as a bedside discriminator with no mention of what else phenylephrine 10 % does — it dilates the pupil and raises blood pressure. **Not taken from the course material**, and flagged only because the bank asks the test four times and never once mentions a caveat.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t7-291` asks this cell as a bare question — \"**Phenylephrine 2.5%** causes blanching in:\" with options conjunctivitis / episcleritis / scleritis / uveitis, keying conjunctivitis. Same table cell, entirely different stem and menu.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Phenylephrine 2.5 % blanches conjunctivitis but not episcleritis, so it is the drop that separates them; 10 % blanches both and therefore differentiates neither.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.50 (Ch.7 Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding episcleritis except?",
    options:["It causes severe eye pain","It causes photophobia","It's usually accompanied by tender episcleral nodules","It may be either simple or nodular"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THIS STEM IS PRINTED TWICE IN THE CHAPTER.** `opmcq-c7-16` carries the identical sentence with four entirely different options and a different key. They are two questions, not a reprint, and any stem-only comparator will score the pair 1.00.\n\n**Three of these options are `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98, and the fourth is the word the course reserves for scleritis.**\n\n> Symptoms: • Acute (simple episcleritis or gradual (nodular) onset of red eye • **Discomfort photophobia or tenderness.**\n> Signs: • Sectoral or diffuse injection • Chemosis • **Tender episcleral nodules** that can be easily mobilized over the sclera\n> Clinical types: **Nodular** … **Simple** (More Common)\n\n| Option | The course's word | True of episcleritis? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Severe eye pain** | Episcleritis gets **discomfort** (p.98), **\"Mild pain and/or discomfort\"** (`L13,14` slide 30), and \"No visual affection\" (p.102). **Severe pain belongs to scleritis** — p.99 gives necrotizing anterior scleritis \"**severe** pain and extreme scleral tenderness\", and `L13,14` slide 34 gives anterior scleritis \"**Moderate to severe** pain\" | ⚠️ **FALSE. The key** |\n| **B. Photophobia** | Printed in the symptom line, and again in p.102's table (\"Irritation: discomfort, **photophobia**\") | True |\n| **C. Tender episcleral nodules** | Printed in the signs list, with the mobility clause | True |\n| **D. Simple or nodular** | The chapter's own two-type classification | True |\n\n**The discrimination is degree, not presence.** Episcleritis is not painless — options B and C both describe genuine discomfort, and the disease is tender by definition. What it is not is **severe**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.102's comparison table draws the line by putting *Irritation / Tenderness* in the episcleritis column and the bare word **Pain** in the scleritis column, and nowhere gives episcleritis an intensity adjective.\n\n⚠️ **A reader who has memorised `opmcq-c7-17` will hesitate over A**, because that question keys \"Scleritis is a painful condition while episcleritis is a **painless** one\" as a correct differentiator. The two questions are consistent only if *painless* is read as shorthand for *not severely painful* — see the note on `opmcq-c7-17`, where the overstatement is recorded.\n\n⚠️ **Option C's word \"usually\" is doing quiet work and it is worth noticing.** Tender nodules are the sign of the **nodular** type; simple episcleritis is defined by p.98 as \"vascular congestion … in the **absence of an obvious nodule**\" and is the *more common* of the two. So \"usually accompanied by tender episcleral nodules\" is, on the book's own frequency statement, arguably false — the commoner type has no nodule. **The bank does not treat it as the exception and the key is not disputed.** It is recorded here because the same reasoning is what makes `opmcq-c7-15` key \"All of the above\" over \"It shows no nodules\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Episcleritis gives discomfort, photophobia, tenderness and mobile nodules and comes in simple and nodular types; severe pain is the scleritis word and never the episcleritis one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.50 (Ch.7 Q6; the identical stem is printed again as Ch.7 Q16 with different options)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Vision loss due to scleritis is related to which of the following prognostic factors?",
    options:["Duration of disease","Site of disease","Severity of disease","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**One printed sentence, and \"All of the above\" is the trap.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100, the prognosis block:\n\n> - **Visual loss is related to the severity of the scleritis.**\n> - Patients with mild or moderate scleritis usually maintain excellent vision\n> - Scleritis may be active for several months or years before going into long-term remission.\n> - Patients with necrotizing scleritis have a high incidence of visual loss and an increased mortality rate.\n\nThe first line is the key verbatim. The second and fourth are the same claim from both ends — mild and moderate disease keeps its vision, necrotizing disease loses it — which is a severity gradient and nothing else.\n\n| Option | Is it in the prognosis block? | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Duration** | ⚠️ Duration **is** mentioned on the page — \"active for several months or years\" — but as a description of the disease course, **not** as a determinant of visual outcome. Nothing links length of illness to vision | The nearest miss |\n| **B. Site** | Anterior and posterior scleritis have different complications (posterior gets serous RD and loss of vision, p.99), so site is not irrelevant — but the book never puts site in the prognosis block | Plausible, unprinted |\n| **C. Severity** | Printed, verbatim, first line | The key |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | Would require A and B to be printed as prognostic factors. Neither is |\n\n**Why D is the option most people choose.** Every clause in A and B is *defensible clinically*, and \"all of the above\" rewards a reader who reasons rather than recalls. This question punishes exactly that: the book names one factor, and the question says \"which of the following prognostic **factors**\" — it is asking what the source printed, not what is medically arguable.\n\n⚠️ **The deck cannot answer this question at all.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` runs slides 34 to 39 on scleritis — anterior, necrotizing, posterior, causes, treatment — and prints **no prognosis line of any kind**. Measured directly: `prognosis` returns zero hits in that file. This is a book-only fact, which is why the citation is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100 alone.\n\n**Its sibling is `opmcq-c7-24`**, which asks the same page's other superlative — pain as the most important indicator of *progression* — and is the question this one is most easily confused with. **Severity determines the outcome; pain tracks the activity.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The book names exactly one prognostic factor for visual loss in scleritis — severity — and supports it from both ends: mild and moderate disease keeps excellent vision, necrotizing disease has a high incidence of visual loss.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.50 (Ch.7 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"All of the following can cause episcleritis except?",
    options:["IBD","SLE","DM","Herpes zoster","Temporal arteritis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Four of these five are on one printed list; the fifth is not on it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98, causes of episcleritis:\n\n> • **Idiopathic (most cases)**\n> • Collagen vascular disease (rheumatoid arthritis, **systemic lupus erythematosus**, seronegative spondyloarthropathies)\n> • Vasculitides (polyarteritis nodosa, **temporal arteritis**, Behçet's disease)\n> • **Inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis)**\n> • Dermatologic disease (Rosacea)\n> • Metabolic disease (gout)\n> • Atopy\n> • Infections (**Herpes zoster**, Herpes simplex, Syphilis)\n\n| Option | Where on the list | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. IBD** | Its own line, with both named diseases | True cause |\n| **B. SLE** | Named inside collagen vascular disease | True cause |\n| **C. DM** | ⚠️ **Absent.** Diabetes appears nowhere in the episcleritis causes, nowhere in the scleritis causes on p.100, and nowhere in `L13,14` slide 32 | **The key** |\n| **D. Herpes zoster** | Named inside infections, on both the book's list and the deck's | True cause |\n| **E. Temporal arteritis** | Named inside vasculitides | True cause |\n\n**The question is really testing whether you can tell an ocular association from a systemic-disease list you half-remember.** Diabetes is the most common systemic disease in any ophthalmology course and it owns a whole chapter of this one — retinopathy, cataract, cranial nerve palsies, rubeosis — so \"DM causes eye disease\" is a reflex. It is not, on this course's material, a cause of episcleritis.\n\n⚠️ **How the absence was measured, because an absence claim has to say how.** `diabet|DM\\b` returns **zero hits** inside the episcleritis and scleritis sections of `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` and zero inside slides 28–40 of `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt`. The book chapter's own verified-absence register makes no mention of it either way, so this is measured from the causes lists themselves rather than from a not-covered block. **The specific thing absent is diabetes mellitus from the two printed causes lists** — not \"diabetes is not taught\", which would be false.\n\n⚠️ **The two causes lists overlap heavily and the differences are examinable.** Episcleritis and scleritis share collagen vascular disease, gout and infections; **episcleritis alone** gets vasculitides, IBD, rosacea and atopy; **scleritis alone** gets chemical or physical injuries, and gives rheumatoid arthritis the explicit rank of \"most common association\" (p.100) where the episcleritis list ranks only **idiopathic** first. That last asymmetry is what `opmcq-c7-18` tests.\n\n⚠️ **The deck's list is shorter than the book's** — `L13,14` slide 32 gives idiopathic, collagen vascular, vasculitis, IBD, rosacea, gout and infections, and **omits atopy** and the named syphilis and herpes simplex. Neither omission changes this key.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The book's episcleritis causes are idiopathic first, then collagen vascular disease, vasculitides, IBD, rosacea, gout, atopy and infection — diabetes is on neither the episcleritis nor the scleritis list.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.50 (Ch.7 Q8)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"A 35-year-old woman has a significant history of recurrent episcleritis. Which of the following would be most appropriate to do for this patient?",
    options:["Prescribe steroids","Increase the dose of NSAIDs","Look for an underlying systemic inflammatory disease","Hospitalize and monitor closely"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The word carrying the question is *recurrent*, and the course attaches one sentence to it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98, in the definition block:\n\n> – **Recurrent** cases may signify the presence of an **underlying systemic inflammatory disease.**\n\nand again on p.99, in management:\n\n> • Patients who experience **recurrent attacks may require systemic evaluation**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 33 closes its episcleritis management list with the identical line. **Three printings of the same instruction across two sources**, and it is the only thing either source says about recurrence. So **C**.\n\n**A single attack of episcleritis needs no workup.** It is \"relatively common, benign, self-limited\" and \"usually clears on its own without treatment\" (p.98, p.99). What changes with recurrence is not the eye — it is the probability that the eye is reporting a systemic disease, and the causes list on p.98 is the differential to work through: rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, seronegative spondyloarthropathies, polyarteritis nodosa, temporal arteritis, Behçet's, Crohn's, ulcerative colitis, rosacea, gout.\n\n| Option | The course's position | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Prescribe steroids** | Topical steroids are genuinely on the p.99 list — but qualified: \"(prescribed and monitored by an **ophthalmologist**)\". And steroids treat the attack; they do nothing about why the attacks keep coming | Treats the symptom, ignores the stem's word |\n| **B. Increase the dose of NSAIDs** | Oral NSAIDs are \"the **main line** of treatment (Ibuprofen 800 mg three times daily)\" — but 800 mg tds is the printed dose, not a starting point to escalate from, and again this is attack treatment | Same fault as A, one step further |\n| **C. Look for an underlying systemic inflammatory disease** | p.98 and p.99, and `L13,14` slide 33 | The key |\n| **D. Hospitalize and monitor closely** | Nothing in either source admits anyone for episcleritis. The disease is benign and self-limited; the only condition in this chapter that generates urgency is **scleritis**, and that gets urgent *referral*, not admission | No |\n\n**A and B are both real treatments, which is what makes them the trap.** The question does not ask what to give her; it asks what is *most appropriate* for a patient defined by recurrence. Every option except C treats this attack as if it were the first one.\n\n⚠️ **The demographics quietly support the reading.** Episcleritis is \"more common in **females** than in males (**3:1**)\" (p.98), and the collagen vascular diseases at the head of its causes list — rheumatoid arthritis, SLE — are themselves female-predominant. A 35-year-old woman with recurrent episcleritis is the exact patient in whom the yield of a systemic workup is highest. *(The female predominance of RA and SLE themselves is **not taken from the course material** — this chapter prints the ocular sex ratio only.)*\n\n⚠️ **`L37) Red Eye.txt` states the rule a fourth time and adds nothing to it**: \"If **recurrent**, it may be associated with an underlying systemic inflammatory disease\" (line 179). It gives no workup, names no test, and the book names none either — **no investigation for episcleritis is printed anywhere in the module.** \"Look for\" is as specific as the course permits, which is why the option could be written as a direction rather than a test.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A single episcleritis attack is benign and self-limited, but recurrent episcleritis may signify an underlying systemic inflammatory disease and calls for systemic evaluation rather than more anti-inflammatory drug.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.50-51 (Ch.7 Q9; the stem is split across the page break — "…recurrent episcleritis. Which" ends p.50 and "of the following would be most appropriate…" opens p.51)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Bluish color of the sclera is noted in which of the following conditions?",
    options:["Marfan syndrome","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Ehlers Danlos syndrome","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The mechanism is printed and it explains all three at once.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.97:\n\n> Sclera is whitish in color but can attain a **bluish** discoloration due to **thinning and subsequent transparency of scleral collagen**, which allows increased visibility of underlying **choroidal pigment**.\n> • This can be seen in some conditions such as:\n>    o **Marfan syndrome**\n>    o **Osteogenesis imperfecta**\n\nBlue sclera is not a pigment — it is the choroid seen through a sclera too thin to hide it. Any disorder that makes scleral collagen thin or abnormal produces it, which is why the answer is a class of disease rather than a list to memorise.\n\n| Option | Where the course puts it | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Marfan syndrome** | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.97, named | Printed |\n| **B. Osteogenesis imperfecta** | p.97, and `L13,14` slide 29 (printed \"Osteogenesis **Ipmerfecta**\" *[sic]*) | Printed in **both** sources — the only one that is |\n| **C. Ehlers Danlos syndrome** | ⚠️ **Not printed anywhere in the module.** True nonetheless — EDS is a heritable disorder of collagen, and the kyphoscoliotic type in particular is described with blue sclerae. **Not taken from the course material** | The gap this question opens |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | The key, and it is correct |\n\n⚠️ **HOW THE ABSENCE OF EHLERS-DANLOS WAS MEASURED.** `ehlers` and `danlos`, case-insensitively, return **exactly two hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters**, and **both are records of absence rather than teaching**: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` line 724 lists \"EHLERS–DANLOS, Marfan and alkaptonuria/ochronosis\" among other causes of blue sclera it does **not** cover, and `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` line 412 records that blue sclera is given \"only two named associations (Marfan syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta) and no others (e.g. Ehlers-Danlos is **not printed**)\". Every hit was opened. The specific thing absent is **the name Ehlers-Danlos from any teaching line in the module** — not blue sclera, which is taught twice.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THE TWO SOURCES DISAGREE ABOUT WHICH DISEASES TO NAME, AND THE DECK'S THIRD NAME IS NOT ON THIS MENU.** `L13,14` slide 29 prints:\n\n> Due to thinning and subsequent transparency of scleral collagen … E.g., **Osteogenesis Ipmerfecta, Buphthalmos**\n\nThe deck gives **buphthalmos** and never names Marfan; the book gives **Marfan** and never names buphthalmos. Only osteogenesis imperfecta is on both. Recorded, not resolved — and worth holding, because buphthalmos is the mechanically obvious case: an infant eye stretched by congenital glaucoma has a thinned sclera for exactly the printed reason.\n\n**Its near-twin is `opmcq-c7-13`**, which asks the same question with a menu of scleritis / episcleritis / osteogenesis imperfecta / scleral tumor and keys the one name both sources print. **`opmcq-c7-13` is answerable from the course alone; this one is not.**\n\n⚠️ **A third association the course does print, and no bank question asks:** p.97's \"Normal Scleral color\" block gives **physiological** blue sclera in children — \"In children, a bluish is observed *[sic — verb missing]* because of the extremely thin sclera which allows the visibility of underlying choroid\" — and yellowish sclera in old age from fat deposition. The same mechanism, no disease attached.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Blue sclera is choroid seen through thinned, transparent scleral collagen; the book names Marfan syndrome and osteogenesis imperfecta, the deck names osteogenesis imperfecta and buphthalmos, and Ehlers-Danlos is a true collagen-disorder cause that the module never prints.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.51 (Ch.7 Q10)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following eye conditions has the highest risk of retinal detachment and subsequent vision loss?",
    options:["Episcleritis","Anterior scleritis","Posterior scleritis","Conjunctivitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Only one of the four is printed with a retinal detachment attached to it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99, posterior scleritis:\n\n> – Inflammation **posterior** to the insertion of the rectus muscles.\n> – **Rare**\n> – Can be associated with **serous retinal detachment and loss of vision** as well as pain upon eye movement.\n> – **U/S:** Scleral thickening ± RD\n\nand p.100 repeats it among the signs: \"With **posterior scleritis**, there may be **serous retinal detachment**\". `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 37 prints the same three bullets. **Two sources, same sentence.**\n\n**The anatomy is why.** Anterior and posterior scleritis are separated by one landmark — the insertion of the rectus muscles — and that landmark decides which structure the inflamed sclera is sitting against. Anterior to it, the sclera abuts cornea, anterior chamber and ciliary body, so p.100 gives anterior disease **keratitis with corneal thinning, uveitis, and trabeculitis (elevated IOP)**. Posterior to it, the sclera abuts choroid and retina, so inflammation there exudes fluid under the retina and lifts it. The detachment is **serous** — an exudative lift, not a break — which matters because it is the type that resolves when the inflammation is controlled rather than needing a retinal operation. *(The serous-vs-rhegmatogenous management contrast is **not taken from the course material**; this chapter names the type and says nothing about treating it.)*\n\n| Option | Its printed complications | Retinal detachment? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Episcleritis** | \"Benign, **self-limited**\", \"**No visual affection**\" (p.98, p.102) | None. It has no printed ocular complication at all |\n| **B. Anterior scleritis** | Keratitis, corneal thinning, uveitis, trabeculitis with raised IOP (p.100) | ⚠️ No — real complications, none retinal |\n| **C. Posterior scleritis** | Serous RD, loss of vision, pain on eye movement, scleral thickening (p.99, p.100) | **The key** |\n| **D. Conjunctivitis** | Not a scleral disease; nothing in chapter 6 detaches a retina | None |\n\n**B is the option to argue with**, because anterior scleritis is by far the commoner disease and the one with the alarming photographs. It damages the eye through the anterior segment. ⚠️ Note the frequency inversion the question quietly relies on: posterior scleritis is printed as **rare** and anterior as **more common**, so the *highest risk* here is a risk per case, not a number of cases. A stem asking \"which causes the most retinal detachments\" would be a different question and the course could not answer it.\n\n⚠️ **Posterior scleritis is the chapter's diagnostic problem, and that is why it owns two questions.** It is the one entity here with **no red eye to look at** — the inflammation is behind the equator — so it is reached by imaging rather than by inspection. `opmcq-c7-12` asks that imaging.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret` — the mechanism is a serous retinal detachment, and retinal detachment is that chapter's subject. Filed to `op-sclera` because the question asks which **scleral** disease carries the risk, and all three plausible options are scleral.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Posterior scleritis inflames the sclera behind the rectus insertions, against choroid and retina, and can produce serous retinal detachment with vision loss and pain on eye movement; anterior scleritis damages the anterior segment instead, and episcleritis has no visual affection.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.51 (Ch.7 Q11)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most suitable imaging modality that can detect posterior scleritis?",
    options:["B-scan U/S","Orbital MRI","CT orbit","A & B together","A & C together"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The book prints both modalities in a single sentence, and the question is whether you noticed the *and*.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100, under Management → Investigations:\n\n> **Ocular B-scan ultrasonography** and **orbital magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)** may be used for the detection of **posterior scleritis**.\n\nThat is the entire investigation section of the chapter — one sentence, two modalities, no CT. So **D**.\n\n| Option | Printed for posterior scleritis? |\n|---|---|\n| **A. B-scan U/S** | Yes — twice. p.100's investigation line, and p.99's \"**U/S:** Scleral thickening ± RD\" | \n| **B. Orbital MRI** | Yes — p.100, named in full as \"orbital magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)\" |\n| **C. CT orbit** | ⚠️ **No.** CT is not named for this indication anywhere in the chapter |\n| **D. A & B together** | **The key** |\n| **E. A & C together** | Fails on C |\n\n**Ultrasound is the one that carries the sign.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99 prints a B-scan panel captioned **\"T\" sign**, with the printed legend *\"B-Scan ultrasonography reveals: severely thickened sclera (a) and a classic T-sign; and anterior nasal choroidal detachments (b).\"* `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 37 prints the same figure with the same caption. ⚠️ **The T sign is taught ONLY in that figure** — the prose on p.99 and p.100 says \"scleral thickening ± RD\" and never names it. The T is fluid in Tenon's space meeting the thickened optic nerve shadow at right angles, and it is the classic posterior-scleritis finding.\n\n⚠️ **THE SPELLING TRAP, RECORDED BECAUSE IT HAS COST THIS PROJECT TIME.** Searching this module for `T-sign` returns nothing: `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` prints **\"T SIGN\"** and **\"T-sign\"** in different places within one caption. Grep both.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THE DECK CONTRADICTS THE BOOK BY OMISSION, AND THE OMISSION IS FORMALLY RECORDED.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` gives posterior scleritis only \"**U/S** : Scleral thickening ± RD\", and its own not-covered register at line 730 states that no investigation other than ultrasound is named — **no CT and no MRI anywhere in the deck**. A candidate revising from slides alone would key **A**, not D. The book is the source of the key here and the divergence is recorded, not corrected.\n\n**Cross-bank contact:** `opqb-t2-83` asks \"**Ultrasonography** is valuable in all of the following **EXCEPT**\" and carries \"Posterior scleritis\" as one of its four options — i.e. it uses this fact as a distractor rather than testing it. That is a one-option overlap, not a shared question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Posterior scleritis is investigated with B-scan ultrasonography and orbital MRI — not CT — and the ultrasound sign to recognise is the T sign, taught only in the figure caption.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.51 (Ch.7 Q12; the fifth option is printed "E. A & C together", the letter being the printed option label)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Bluish discoloration of the eye sclera occurs in which of the following conditions?",
    options:["Scleritis","Episcleritis","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Scleral tumor"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The one name both course sources print.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.97 lists Marfan syndrome and **osteogenesis imperfecta** under blue sclera; `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 29 lists \"**Osteogenesis Ipmerfecta** *[sic]*, Buphthalmos\". Osteogenesis imperfecta is the intersection, and it is the only option on this menu that appears on either list. The mechanism — choroid visible through thinned, transparent scleral collagen — is written out in full at `opmcq-c7-10`, which asks the same fact with a wider menu.\n\n⚠️ **THE REAL DIFFICULTY IS OPTION A, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN IT LOOKS.** Scleritis *does* turn an eye blue-violet. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100 prints it as a **sign**: \"The redness has a characteristic **violet-bluish** hue with scleral oedema\", and p.99's Tutorial box explains it — \"Engorgement of the deep episcleral plexus\" plus \"Scleral thinning\". So there are **two distinct routes to a bluish eye in this one chapter**, and the question turns on which one the stem describes:\n\n| | Blue sclera | Scleritis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What is blue** | The sclera itself, **discoloured** | The inflamed eye, a **violet-bluish hue over redness** |\n| **Mechanism** | Thin transparent collagen → **choroid shows through** | Engorged **deep episcleral plexus** ± scleral thinning |\n| **The eye is otherwise** | White, quiet, painless, chronic | Red, oedematous, **painful**, tender |\n| **Where the book puts it** | p.97, **anatomy** section | p.100, scleritis **signs** |\n\nThe stem says \"bluish **discoloration**\" and describes no inflammation at all — no pain, no redness, no oedema. That is the p.97 entity, not the p.100 sign. **A would need the stem to describe a red painful eye, and it describes nothing of the kind.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Scleritis** | ⚠️ Produces a violet-bluish **hue on an inflamed red eye**, which is a different finding from discoloration of a quiet sclera. The strongest distractor in the chapter |\n| **B. Episcleritis** | No colour change of any kind is printed for it. Its injection is bright and superficial; p.102 gives it \"No visual affection\" and no hue |\n| **C. Osteogenesis imperfecta** | **The key** |\n| **D. Scleral tumor** | ⚠️ Not an entity in this course. `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` line 415 records \"**No scleral tumours**\" among the chapter's verified absences, and the term appears in no lecture deck. A pure invention distractor |\n\n**Near-twin: `opmcq-c7-10`**, four pages earlier, asks the same fact over Marfan / OI / Ehlers-Danlos / all of the above and keys \"All of the above\". ⚠️ **The two are not a reprint** — different menus, different keys, and a different demand: `opmcq-c7-10` needs outside knowledge (Ehlers-Danlos is nowhere in the module), while **this one is answerable from the course alone**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Osteogenesis imperfecta is the blue-sclera cause both course sources print; scleritis gives a violet-bluish hue over an inflamed painful red eye, which is a sign of inflammation rather than discoloration of a quiet sclera.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.51 (Ch.7 Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following has the worst prognosis ever?",
    options:["Episcleritis","Scleritis","Conjunctivitis","Subconjunctival hemorrhage"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **\"ever\" is printed and is kept.** It is not a typo for a recoverable word, and the stem gives no organ, no patient and no context — so the four options are the whole question, and it resolves to: which of these four red eyes is the dangerous one?\n\n**Only one of the four is called vision-threatening anywhere in the course.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100 and p.102:\n\n> Scleritis is a **vision-threatening** condition that requires urgent referral to an ophthalmologist.\n> **Visual loss** is related to the **severity** of the scleritis … Patients with **necrotizing scleritis** have a high incidence of visual loss and an **increased mortality rate**.\n\n| Option | The course's prognosis for it | Rank |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Episcleritis** | \"**Self-limited**\", \"usually clears on its own without treatment\", \"**No visual affection**\" (pp.98, 99, 102). p.102's prognosis cell reads simply **Self-limited** | Benign |\n| **B. Scleritis** | Vision-threatening; visual loss with severity; **increased mortality** in the necrotizing form | **The key** |\n| **C. Conjunctivitis** | Chapter 6's diseases are self-limiting or antibiotic-responsive; nothing in it is called vision-threatening | Benign |\n| **D. Subconjunctival hemorrhage** | Blood under a transparent membrane. Alarming to look at, resolves untreated | ⚠️ The **look-versus-danger** distractor — the most dramatic photograph on the list and the least consequential disease |\n\n**Scleritis is the answer because of what the sclera is.** It is the eye's structural coat — \"**5/6** of the outer fibrous protective coat\" (p.97) — so inflaming it threatens the globe's integrity, not just its surface. That is why the treatment list ends with surgery \"in cases of **scleral perforation** or the presence of excessive scleral thinning with a **high risk of rupture**\" (p.100), and why the necrotizing form carries a mortality figure: the systemic vasculitis that eats the sclera is also eating other organs.\n\n⚠️ **The mortality line is the fact worth taking from this question**, and it is easy to skim past. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100 and p.102 both print that necrotizing scleritis carries an **increased mortality rate** — the only mention of death anywhere in the chapter, and one of very few in the module. The eye is a marker of systemic disease severity here. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` prints **no prognosis line at all** for either disease, so this is a book-only fact.\n\n**Its narrower sibling is `opmcq-c7-20`**, which asks the same superlative *within* scleritis and keys the necrotizing form. **Read as a pair: scleritis is the worst of the red eyes, and necrotizing scleritis is the worst of the scleritides.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Of the common red eyes, only scleritis is called vision-threatening; episcleritis is self-limited with no visual affection, conjunctivitis and subconjunctival haemorrhage are benign, and necrotizing scleritis adds an increased mortality rate.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.52 (Ch.7 Q14; the stem is printed "worst prognosis ever")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following applies to simple episcleritis?",
    options:["It is the most common type of episcleritis","It shows no nodules","It's either sectoral or diffuse","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three statements are one printed block.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98, clinical types of episcleritis:\n\n> **Nodular:**\n>  • A discrete elevated area of inflamed episcleral tissue is present (Less acute onset and more prolonged course)\n> **Simple:** (**More Common**)\n>  • Vascular congestion is present in the **absence of an obvious nodule.**\n>  • It can be either:\n>     o **Sectoral** (2/3 of cases)\n>     o **Diffuse** (1/3 of cases)\n\nEach of the three options is one line of that block, so **D**.\n\n| Option | Its line | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Most common type** | \"Simple: (**More Common**)\" — the book's own parenthesis | Yes |\n| **B. Shows no nodules** | \"in the **absence of an obvious nodule**\" | Yes — and this is the *definition* of the type, not an incidental feature |\n| **C. Sectoral or diffuse** | The two sub-types, with 2/3 and 1/3 | Yes |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | **The key** |\n| **E. None of the above** | — | Fails |\n\n⚠️ **THE FRACTIONS ARE THE EXAMINABLE PART AND THIS QUESTION DOES NOT ASK THEM.** Simple episcleritis is **sectoral in 2/3** and **diffuse in 1/3** — Grade Gain does ask exactly that, and its key is *simple sectoral* (`opqb-t7-293`). ⚠️ Watch the arithmetic trap the two fractions set up alongside the chapter's other 2/3: p.98 also prints that **2/3 of episcleritis cases are unilateral**. **Two different 2/3s on one page, about two different things.**\n\n⚠️ **The deck teaches the same split with the sub-types collapsed into the type name.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 31 prints only \"Simple (Sectoral or diffuse injection)\" and \"Nodular\", **with no frequency for either** — the \"More Common\" tag and both fractions are the book's alone. A slide-only reader could answer B and C here but not A.\n\n⚠️ **Note the tension with `opmcq-c7-6`.** That question keeps \"It's usually accompanied by tender episcleral nodules\" as a **true** statement about episcleritis in general, while this one keys the *commonest type* as having **no** nodule. Both keys stand as printed; the reconciliation is that tender mobile nodules are the sign of the nodular type, which the book describes in more detail while calling it the less common one. Recorded, neither key disputed.\n\n**The onset difference is the other half of the classification and neither bank question asks it:** simple episcleritis is **acute** in onset, nodular is **gradual** with \"less acute onset and more prolonged course\" (p.98).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Simple episcleritis is the commoner of the two types, is defined by the absence of a nodule, and is sectoral in two-thirds and diffuse in one-third; the nodular type has a less acute onset and a longer course.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.52 (Ch.7 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding episcleritis except?",
    options:["It's mostly unilateral","It mostly affects females","It's mostly self-limited","It's an uncommon condition"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THIS STEM IS PRINTED TWICE IN THE CHAPTER** — `opmcq-c7-6` carries the identical sentence with four different options and a different key. Two questions, not a reprint.\n\n**Three of these four options are one printed line each.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98:\n\n> – Relatively **common**, benign, **self-limited** cause of red eye.\n> – **(2/3)** of cases are **unilateral**\n> – More common in **females** than in males **(3:1)**\n\n| Option | The line | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Mostly unilateral** | \"2/3 of cases are unilateral\" | True — and *mostly* is the right hedge for two-thirds |\n| **B. Mostly affects females** | \"More common in females than in males (3:1)\" | True |\n| **C. Mostly self-limited** | \"benign, self-limited\", and p.99 \"usually clears on its own without treatment\" | True |\n| **D. An uncommon condition** | \"**Relatively common**\" | ⚠️ **FALSE — the key** |\n\n**The pattern is this bank's standard *except* construction**: three verbatim lifts and one lift with its polarity flipped.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **A THREE-WAY SOURCE SPLIT SITS EXACTLY ON THE KEYED WORD, AND IT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN CORRECTED.** `L37) Red Eye.txt` — which is a **book chapter carrying real prose**, not a slide deck — says at line 178:\n\n> Episcleritis is an inflammation (often sectoral) of the episclera … The condition is **uncommon** and has the following features: no discharge, not vision threatening, and often tender over the inflamed area.\n\nThat is the keyed option, stated as fact, in a source this module treats as authoritative. Against it, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98 prints \"**Relatively common**\" and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 30 prints \"**Relatively common, benign and self-limited cause of red eye**\". **Two sources to one**, and the two that agree are the module's own textbook and its own lecture. `L37` also calls **scleritis** \"uncommon\" (line 168), so its scale is set against all red eyes rather than against scleritis specifically — which is the likeliest reconciliation, but it is a reading, not something either source states. **The key is not disputed.**\n\n⚠️ **`L37` disagrees a second time on the same disease.** It says episcleritis is \"**usually not associated with serious systemic disease**\" (figure 4-9 caption), where `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98 and `L13,14` slide 33 both make recurrent episcleritis a reason to hunt for systemic inflammatory disease — the fact `opmcq-c7-9` keys. The two are compatible (most cases are idiopathic; recurrent ones are the exception) but the emphasis is opposite.\n\n⚠️ **The 3:1 sex ratio is the number to carry from option B**, and it belongs to episcleritis. Scleritis has its own demographic and it is different: \"more common in **middle aged females**\" (`L13,14` slide 34; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99 prints the same inside a Tutorial marker). ⚠️ Note that **p.102's comparison table leaves the scleritis Sex cell BLANK** — the book gives a ratio for episcleritis and none for scleritis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Episcleritis is relatively common, unilateral in two-thirds, three times commoner in females and self-limited — and the module's Red Eye chapter is the one source that calls it uncommon.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.52 (Ch.7 Q16; the identical stem is printed earlier as Ch.7 Q6 with different options)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following can differentiate scleritis from episcleritis?",
    options:["Scleritis is a painful condition while episcleritis is a painless one","Phenylephrine 10% causes redness of episcleritis to disappear","Both A & B","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Both limbs are printed differentiators, so the key is C.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.102 sets the two diseases side by side, and the symptom row is the first limb:\n\n| | Episcleritis | Scleritis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Symptoms** | Red eye · **Irritation**: discomfort, photophobia · **Tenderness** · **No visual affection** | Red eye · **Pain** · **Decreased vision** may be present |\n| *(unlabelled row)* | **Blanching** occurs with **phenylephrine 10%** | **No blanching** occurs with **phenylephrine 10%** (D.D. from episcleritis) |\n\nThe table puts the bare word **Pain** in the scleritis column and never puts it in the episcleritis column — that is limb A. The row beneath is limb B, and the book labels it *D.D. from episcleritis* in so many words, i.e. it is telling you outright that this is the discriminator.\n\n⚠️ **LIMB A OVERSTATES THE COURSE, AND THE OVERSTATEMENT IS RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED.** Episcleritis is **not** strictly painless. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.98 gives it \"**Discomfort** photophobia or **tenderness**\", p.99 refers to \"cases with **pain** or discomfort\", and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 30 prints \"**Mild pain** and/or discomfort\" and \"Mild localised ocular **tenderness**\". A source that says *mild pain* cannot also mean *painless*. What the sources do support is a difference of **degree** — mild discomfort against \"**Moderate to severe** pain with globe tenderness\" (`L13,14` slide 34) and \"**severe** pain and extreme scleral tenderness\" in the necrotizing form (p.99). **The printed key is not disputed**; option A is best read as *painful versus not-significantly-painful*.\n\n⚠️ **This is exactly the tension that `opmcq-c7-6` creates from the other side**, where \"It causes severe eye pain\" is keyed as the FALSE statement about episcleritis. Both keys are consistent only on the degree reading: episcleritis has mild pain, so *severe pain* is false of it (`opmcq-c7-6`) and *painful versus painless* still separates it from scleritis (here).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Pain vs painless** | A real differentiator by degree; ⚠️ literally false as printed |\n| **B. Phenylephrine 10 % blanches episcleritis** | Exactly right, and the book's own labelled D.D. — the same cell `opmcq-c7-4` keys |\n| **C. Both A & B** | **The key** |\n| **D. Neither A nor B** | Fails; B alone is unarguable |\n\n**A third differentiator neither limb mentions, and it is the one a clinician actually uses:** the **hue**. Episcleritis is bright red and superficial; scleritis carries \"a characteristic **violet-bluish** hue with scleral oedema\" from engorgement of the **deep** episcleral plexus (p.99, p.100). ⚠️ And a fourth, from `opmcq-c7-1`: episcleral vessels **move** over the sclera under a cotton bud, deep scleral vessels do not. **Four discriminators; the bank asks two.**\n\n**Third phenylephrine question in the chapter** (`opmcq-c7-4`, `opmcq-c7-5`, this one), and `opmcq-c7-22` makes a fourth appearance as a distractor. The full 3×2 table is written out at `opmcq-c7-4`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Scleritis is separated from episcleritis by pain and by failure to blanch with phenylephrine 10 %; the course gives episcleritis mild pain rather than none, and adds the violet-bluish hue and immobile deep vessels as further discriminators.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.52 (Ch.7 Q17)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following is most commonly associated with scleritis?",
    options:["Gout","TB","Herpes zoster","Rheumatoid arthritis","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **FOUR OF THE FIVE OPTIONS ARE REAL CAUSES, WHICH IS WHY \"most commonly\" IS THE ENTIRE QUESTION.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100 and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 38 print the same list, and both open it with a ranking:\n\n> • **Rheumatoid arthritis is the most common association** as well as other collagen vascular diseases (systemic lupus erythematosus, seronegative spondyloarthropathies)\n> • Metabolic disease (**gout**)\n> • Infections (**TB**, Syphilis, **Herpes zoster**, ...etc)\n> • Chemical or physical injuries\n> • idiopathic\n\n| Option | On the list? | Rank |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Gout** | Yes, as metabolic disease | Unranked |\n| **B. TB** | Yes, first of the named infections | Unranked |\n| **C. Herpes zoster** | Yes, and it causes episcleritis too (p.98) | Unranked |\n| **D. Rheumatoid arthritis** | Yes, **and the only item either source ranks** | **The key** |\n| **E. None of the above** | — | Fails |\n\n**A question that offers four true options and asks for a superlative is testing whether you read the source's own ranking sentence or just its bullet list.** Both sources rank exactly one item, in the same words, at the top of the list.\n\n⚠️ **THE ASYMMETRY WITH EPISCLERITIS IS THE FACT BEHIND THE FACT.** The two causes lists overlap almost entirely, but they rank differently:\n\n| | Episcleritis (p.98) | Scleritis (p.100) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ranked first** | **Idiopathic (most cases)** | **Rheumatoid arthritis** (most common association) |\n| Shared | Collagen vascular · gout · infections | Collagen vascular · gout · infections |\n| Only there | Vasculitides · IBD · rosacea · atopy | Chemical or physical injuries |\n\n**Episcleritis is usually nobody's fault; scleritis usually has a rheumatologist attached.** That is why `opmcq-c7-9` sends a *recurrent* episcleritis for systemic evaluation while scleritis is assumed systemic from the outset. Grade Gain keys the episcleritis half of this contrast in `opqb-t7-296`, which asks the commonest cause in an episcleritis vignette and answers **idiopathic**.\n\n⚠️ **Rheumatoid arthritis also owns the chapter's worst outcome.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99's Tutorial box gives anterior scleritis \"± History of autoimmune disease (**rheumatoid arthritis**)\", and `opqb-t7-294` builds its necrotizing-scleritis vignette on \"a 61-year-old woman with severe **RA**\". The association is not just the commonest — it is the one that produces scleromalacia perforans.\n\n⚠️ **A caution on the word *association*.** Both sources say associated, not caused, and this is the one place the chapter is careful about it: scleritis is a marker of active systemic disease rather than a complication with a mechanism. The book never explains how RA inflames a sclera. *(That there is no printed mechanism is a statement about this chapter, measured by reading pp.96–102 in full.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Gout, TB and herpes zoster are all printed causes of scleritis, but rheumatoid arthritis is the only one either source ranks — it is the most common association, where episcleritis's ranked first cause is idiopathic.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.52 (Ch.7 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding sclera except?",
    options:["It constitutes 5/6 of outer fibrous protective coat of the globe","It is composed of regularly-distributed collagen bundles","It is whitish in color","It is continuous with cornea anteriorly and with dura matter covering optic nerve posteriorly"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The four options are the book's four anatomy bullets, with one word inverted.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.97:\n\n> • The sclera \"the **white** part of the eye\" constitutes **5/6** of the **outer fibrous protective coat** of the globe.\n> • Composed of **irregularly** distributed collagen bundles (therefore **opaque**)\n> • Continuous with the **cornea anteriorly** and with the **dura mater covering the optic nerve posteriorly**.\n> • The episclera is a thin layer of vascularized connective tissue covering the outer surface of the sclera.\n\nThe bank has lifted bullets 1, 2 and 3 verbatim and changed **irregularly** to **regularly**. So **B**.\n\n⚠️ **THE PARENTHESIS THE BANK DELETED IS THE WHOLE REASON THE ANSWER IS B.** The book writes \"irregularly distributed collagen bundles (**therefore opaque**)\", and that clause is the single most useful sentence in the chapter, because it makes the sclera and the cornea one fact instead of two:\n\n| | Collagen arrangement | Optical result |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cornea** | **Regular**, uniformly spaced lamellae | **Transparent** — light passes without scatter |\n| **Sclera** | **Irregular** bundles of varying diameter | **Opaque, white** — all wavelengths scattered |\n\np.97 says so directly in its own colour section: the sclera is white \"because of the **scattering of all wave lengths** of light by dense **irregular** bundles of collagen\". **Option B is therefore not merely unprinted — it would make the sclera transparent**, and options A and C could not both be true alongside it. An *except* question in which the false option contradicts two of the true ones is unusually clean.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. 5/6 of the outer fibrous coat** | True. The cornea is the other **1/6** — p.97's figure is annotated with both fractions |\n| **B. Regularly-distributed collagen bundles** | ⚠️ **FALSE — the key.** Irregular, and that is why it is white |\n| **C. Whitish in color** | True; the book opens with \"the white part of the eye\" |\n| **D. Continuous with cornea anteriorly, dura posteriorly** | True, verbatim |\n\n⚠️ **\"dura matter\" is a misprint for \"dura mater\", and it is the BANK's, not the book's** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.97 prints *mater*. Kept verbatim above; it does not touch the meaning, and the continuity itself is real and clinically loaded: it is why optic nerve sheath and sclera are one connective-tissue system, and why raised intracranial pressure transmits along it.\n\n⚠️ **THIS IS THE CHAPTER'S ONLY ANATOMY QUESTION, AND THE ANATOMY IS FOUR BULLETS LONG.** `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` records the absence precisely: \"No scleral anatomy beyond four bullet points — no lamina cribrosa, no scleral spur, no emissary channels, no episcleral vascular plexus anatomy … no blood or nerve supply, no thickness measurements anywhere.\" ⚠️ **And the deck is worse**: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 28 is a bare section title and slide 29 goes straight to blue sclera — **the deck teaches no scleral anatomy at all**. Every option here is answerable only from the book.\n\n⚠️ **The fifth bullet the bank did not use** defines the **episclera** — \"a thin layer of vascularized connective tissue covering the outer surface of the sclera\" — which is the structure five other questions in this chapter depend on and none of them defines.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The sclera is 5/6 of the outer fibrous coat, white, and continuous with cornea in front and optic nerve dura behind; its collagen bundles are irregularly arranged, which is precisely why it scatters light and is opaque where the regularly-arranged cornea is clear.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.53 (Ch.7 Q19; option D is printed "dura matter", where the book prints "dura mater")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following conditions has the worst prognosis?",
    options:["Diffuse scleritis","Nodular scleritis","Necrotizing scleritis","Episcleritis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`opmcq-c7-14`'s superlative narrowed to the subtypes, and the book ranks them explicitly.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99 and p.100:\n\n> – **Necrotizing anterior scleritis is the most severe form**:\n>    o Characterized by **severe pain** and **extreme scleral tenderness**.\n>    o With or without necrosis and **exposure of the choroid**.\n>    o **Scleromalacia perforans** is a rare form of necrotizing anterior scleritis **without pain**.\n>\n> - Patients with **necrotizing scleritis** have a **high incidence of visual loss** and an **increased mortality rate**.\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 36 prints the same ranking — \"**the most severe form**, with necrosis and exposure of the choroid\". **Two sources, same superlative.** So **C**.\n\n| Option | What the course says about it | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Diffuse scleritis** | ⚠️ Named once, in a three-word list — \"Diffuse, nodular, or necrotizing in appearance\" (p.99) — and never described again | No features, no prognosis |\n| **B. Nodular scleritis** | ⚠️ Same: named in that list and nowhere else | No features, no prognosis |\n| **C. Necrotizing scleritis** | \"Most severe form\", severe pain, extreme tenderness, choroidal exposure, high visual loss, **increased mortality** | **The key** |\n| **D. Episcleritis** | \"Self-limited\", \"No visual affection\" (p.98, p.102) | The benign end of the entire chapter |\n\n⚠️ **THE COURSE TEACHES THIS CLASSIFICATION ASYMMETRICALLY, AND KNOWING THAT IS PART OF ANSWERING IT.** `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` records the absence in terms: \"No **diffuse** anterior scleritis described in its own right — it is named once in the p.99 list … and never given features, unlike the necrotizing form\", and the same for the nodular type. So options A and B are not distractors the course could argue for or against — **the course says nothing about them at all**. Only C carries any prognostic statement, which makes this question answerable by elimination even without the ranking sentence.\n\n**Scleromalacia perforans is the trap this question sets for the next reader.** It is a *rare form of necrotizing anterior scleritis* — a subset of the key, not a fifth option — and it is **painless**, which inverts the cardinal feature of the parent disease. A necrotizing scleritis that does not hurt is not a milder one: the sclera thins to translucency with the uvea showing through, and `ophthalmology.pdf` Fig 7-4 shows a quiet-looking eye that is structurally failing. `opmcq-c7-21` tests exactly this.\n\n⚠️ **The mortality figure is the reason this ranking matters beyond the eye.** Necrotizing scleritis is the ocular face of an active systemic vasculitis — most often rheumatoid, per p.100's causes list — and both p.100 and p.102 attach an **increased mortality rate** to it. It is the only condition in the chapter with a mortality statement, and `L13,14` prints none.\n\n**Read with `opmcq-c7-14`:** scleritis is the worst of the red eyes; necrotizing scleritis is the worst of the scleritides.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Necrotizing anterior scleritis is the most severe form in both sources — severe pain, extreme tenderness, choroidal exposure, high visual loss and increased mortality — while the diffuse and nodular forms are named in a list and never characterised.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.53 (Ch.7 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding necrotizing anterior scleritis except?",
    options:["It is characterized by severe pain and extreme scleral tenderness","It's always associated with gross necrosis and exposure of the choroid","Scleromalacia perforans is a rare form of necrotizing anterior scleritis without pain","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The key turns on one word — *always* — and the book anticipated it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99:\n\n> – **Necrotizing anterior scleritis** is the most severe form:\n>    o Characterized by **severe pain and extreme scleral tenderness**.\n>    o **With or without** necrosis and exposure of the choroid.\n>    o **Scleromalacia perforans** is a rare form of necrotizing anterior scleritis **without pain**.\n\nOptions A and C are those first and third lines verbatim. Option B is the second line with \"with or without\" replaced by \"**always**\". So **B**.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THE TWO SOURCES DIVERGE EXACTLY ON THE KEYED WORD, AND THE DECK ALONE WOULD NOT SUPPORT THIS KEY.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 36 prints:\n\n> Necrotizing anterior scleritis is the most severe form, **with** necrosis and exposure of the choroid.\n\nNo *or without*. Read from the slide, option B is a fair paraphrase and the question has no answer. Read from the book, the hedge is explicit and B is plainly false. **The BOOK supports the printed key; the divergence is recorded and neither source is corrected.** This is the second divergence of exactly this shape in the chapter — see `opmcq-c7-12`, where the deck omits MRI.\n\n| Option | Source line | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Severe pain and extreme scleral tenderness** | p.99, verbatim | True — and it is what makes scleromalacia perforans remarkable |\n| **B. **Always** associated with gross necrosis and exposure of the choroid** | p.99 says \"**with or without**\" | ⚠️ **FALSE — the key** |\n| **C. Scleromalacia perforans is a rare painless form** | p.99, verbatim | True |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | Fails, since B is false |\n\n**A and C look like a contradiction and are not, and that is the real teaching here.** A says the disease is characterised by severe pain; C says one of its forms has none. Both are printed, and the resolution is that **scleromalacia perforans is the exception the book carves out by name** — a rare, painless, quietly destructive variant, classically in long-standing rheumatoid arthritis. `ophthalmology.pdf` Fig 7-4 shows it: a thinned, translucent grey-blue scleral area with uvea visible through it, in an eye that looks quiet. `L13,14` slide 36 shows the same pair of photographs — an intensely inflamed globe with a white avascular necrotic patch above, and the quiet translucent eye below.\n\n⚠️ **The painlessness is the danger, not a reassurance.** A patient whose sclera is melting without pain has no symptom to bring them in, and the eye can reach perforation before anyone looks. That is the point of naming it separately. *(The clinical inference is **not taken from the course material** — both sources state the painlessness and neither draws the consequence.)*\n\n⚠️ **What the course does NOT subdivide**, recorded so it is not invented: `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` states there is \"**No necrotizing scleritis subdivision into with-inflammation vs without-inflammation**\" and no **surgically-induced necrotizing scleritis (SINS)** anywhere. Scleromalacia perforans is the only sub-distinction printed.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t7-294` gives this disease as a vignette — a 61-year-old woman with severe RA, excruciating pain, scleral thinning, exposed uveal tissue and a violaceous hue — and keys **necrotizing scleritis** over scleromalacia perforans, the discriminator being the excruciating pain. **That pair is the clinical version of the A-versus-C tension above.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Necrotizing anterior scleritis is the most severe form, with severe pain and extreme tenderness, but the book prints necrosis and choroidal exposure as present with or without — and scleromalacia perforans is its rare painless variant.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.53 (Ch.7 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"All of the following are typical features of scleritis except?",
    options:["Red eye","Sudden severe vision loss","Ocular pain","No blanching with phenylephrine 10%"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three options are the book's scleritis lines; the fourth changes both the tempo and the degree.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100:\n\n> Symptoms:\n> o **Gradual onset** of ocular pain with globe tenderness (pain is the most important indicator for disease progression)\n> o **Red eye** (localized or diffuse)\n> o **Decreased vision MAY be present**\n>\n> Signs: … **No blanching** occurs with **phenylephrine 10%** (D.D. from episcleritis)\n\n| Option | The book's line | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Red eye** | \"Red eye (localized or diffuse)\" | True |\n| **B. Sudden severe vision loss** | ⚠️ **Two words wrong.** The onset is \"**gradual**\", and the vision statement is \"decreased vision **may** be present\" — a hedge, not a severity | **FALSE — the key** |\n| **C. Ocular pain** | \"Gradual onset of **ocular pain** with globe tenderness\" | True, and it is the disease's cardinal symptom |\n| **D. No blanching with phenylephrine 10%** | Printed as a sign and labelled *D.D. from episcleritis* | True |\n\n**Option B fails twice over, and either failure alone would settle it.** *Sudden* contradicts *gradual onset*; *severe vision loss* contradicts *may be present*. A question whose false option carries two independent errors is a gift, but it also rewards a reader who knows what **does** cause sudden severe visual loss — and none of it is here.\n\n⚠️ **The distinction matters clinically, not just as a matching exercise.** Scleritis threatens vision over weeks to months, through corneal thinning, uveitis, trabeculitis and — posteriorly — serous retinal detachment (p.100). It is dangerous **because it is protracted and destructive**, not because it strikes suddenly: p.100 notes scleritis \"may be active for **several months or years** before going into long-term remission\". Sudden severe visual loss belongs to a different set of diagnoses altogether — vascular occlusion, retinal detachment with the macula off, acute angle-closure. *(That list is **not taken from the course material** in this chapter; `op-red` and the acute-visual-loss lectures carry it.)*\n\n⚠️ **Fourth appearance of the phenylephrine table in twenty-six questions**, and this time as a distractor rather than a stem — see `opmcq-c7-4` for the full 3×2 grid. Option D is one of the safest true statements in the chapter, and a reader hunting for the exception can waste time on it precisely because it is the most technical-sounding option on the list.\n\n⚠️ **The deck cannot fully answer this either.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 34 gives anterior scleritis \"Moderate to severe pain with globe tenderness\" and slide 35 gives \"Localised or diffuse redness\" — so A and C are supported — but the deck prints **no statement about visual acuity at all** for anterior scleritis, and the phenylephrine table on slide 40 covers D. The *gradual onset* and *may be present* wording that makes B false is the **book's alone** (p.100).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Scleritis is a gradual-onset painful red eye in which decreased vision may be present and phenylephrine 10 % produces no blanching; sudden severe visual loss is not its pattern, because it damages vision slowly over months.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.53 (Ch.7 Q22)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Anterior scleritis is characterized by all of the following except?",
    options:["Inflammation occurs anterior to origin of rectus muscles","It's more common than posterior scleritis","It may be either diffuse, nodular or necrotizing","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THE KEY TURNS ON A SINGLE WORD AND IT IS EASY TO READ STRAIGHT PAST.** The bank prints \"anterior to **origin** of rectus muscles\". Both sources print **insertion**:\n\n> `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99 — \"Inflammation anterior to the **insertion** of the rectus muscles\"\n> `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 34 — \"Inflammation anterior to the **insertion** of the rectus muscles.\"\n\nand the posterior form is defined against the same landmark: \"Inflammation **posterior** to the insertion of the rectus muscles\" (p.99, slide 37). So **A**.\n\n**The two words are at opposite ends of the orbit, and swapping them destroys the classification.** The recti **originate** at the annulus of Zinn at the orbital apex and **insert** on the globe a few millimetres behind the limbus. \"Anterior to the origin\" therefore means *anterior to the orbital apex* — which is the whole eyeball, anterior and posterior sclera together, so it would classify nothing. \"Anterior to the insertion\" cuts the globe just behind the limbus, which is exactly the line the disease respects. *(The anatomical position of the annulus of Zinn is **not taken from the course material** — this chapter names the insertion as a landmark and never describes the muscles.)*\n\n| Option | Source | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Anterior to **origin** of rectus muscles** | Both sources print **insertion** | ⚠️ **FALSE — the key** |\n| **B. More common than posterior scleritis** | p.99 gives anterior \"**More common**\" and posterior \"**Rare**\"; p.102's table repeats \"(more common)\" / \"(rare)\" | True |\n| **C. Diffuse, nodular or necrotizing** | p.99, verbatim: \"**Diffuse, nodular, or necrotizing** in appearance\" | True |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | Fails |\n\n**Why the insertion is the right landmark is worth one line:** it is where the visible eye stops. Sclera in front of the rectus insertions is sclera you can see and photograph under a slit lamp; sclera behind it is hidden, which is why anterior scleritis presents as a red eye and **posterior scleritis has to be imaged** (`opmcq-c7-12`) and can masquerade as something else entirely.\n\n⚠️ **Option C is the classification whose members the course never describes** — see `opmcq-c7-20`. Diffuse and nodular anterior scleritis are named in that three-word list and given no features anywhere in either source; only the necrotizing form is described. **The list is examinable, its members are not.**\n\n⚠️ **The frequency claim in option B has a second layer the bank does not test.** Anterior scleritis is not only commoner — p.99 adds \"in **middle aged females**\", marked as Tutorial content in the book and printed plainly on `L13,14` slide 34 as \"More common in middle aged female\". ⚠️ Note that `ophthalmology.pdf` p.102's comparison table leaves the **Sex** cell for scleritis **blank** while giving episcleritis 3:1, so the demographic survives only in the p.99 line and the slide.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Anterior scleritis is inflammation anterior to the INSERTION of the rectus muscles — not their origin, which lies at the orbital apex — and it is commoner than the rare posterior form and appears as diffuse, nodular or necrotizing disease.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.53 (Ch.7 Q23; option A is printed "anterior to origin of rectus muscles", where both course sources print "insertion")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most important indicator for progression of scleritis?",
    options:["Long duration of the disease","Emergence of complications on top of the primary disease","Pain related to the disease","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A parenthesis, asked as a question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.100, first symptom bullet:\n\n> o Gradual onset of ocular pain with globe tenderness (**pain is the most important indicator for disease progression**)\n\nThat is the key, verbatim, and it is the only sentence in the chapter that uses the phrase. So **C**.\n\n| Option | Printed as an indicator of progression? | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Long duration** | ⚠️ Duration appears on p.100 — \"may be active for several months or years\" — but as a description of the natural history, never as a marker of progression | The nearest miss |\n| **B. Emergence of complications** | Complications are listed (keratitis, corneal thinning, uveitis, trabeculitis, serous RD) but never as an index of activity | Clinically reasonable, unprinted |\n| **C. Pain** | p.100, in the book's own parenthesis | **The key** |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | Would need A and B printed as indicators. Neither is |\n| **E. None of the above** | — | Fails |\n\n⚠️ **DO NOT CONFUSE THIS WITH `opmcq-c7-7`.** The same page carries two different superlatives about scleritis and the bank asks both:\n\n| Question | The book's sentence | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| What determines **visual loss**? | \"Visual loss is related to the **severity** of the scleritis\" (prognosis block) | **Severity** |\n| What indicates **progression**? | \"**pain** is the most important indicator for disease progression\" (symptoms block) | **Pain** |\n\n**Severity predicts the outcome; pain tracks the activity.** Mixing them is the commonest way to get one of the two wrong, and both are single printed sentences on p.100.\n\n**Why pain is the right marker makes sense of the whole chapter.** Pain is what separates scleritis from episcleritis in the first place (`opmcq-c7-17`), it escalates with the necrotizing form (\"severe pain and extreme scleral tenderness\", p.99), and it is the symptom the patient reports without being examined — so a scleritis whose pain is increasing is a scleritis that is advancing, regardless of how the eye looks. ⚠️ **And the exception proves the rule dangerously**: **scleromalacia perforans is painless** and is nonetheless the destructive end of the necrotizing spectrum, so in that one variant the indicator fails completely. *(The inference that the indicator fails in scleromalacia perforans is **not taken from the course material** — the book prints both facts and never connects them.)*\n\n⚠️ **The deck prints no equivalent sentence.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 34 gives anterior scleritis \"Moderate to severe pain with globe tenderness\" and stops; there is no progression indicator, and no prognosis line, anywhere in slides 34–39. Book-only, like `opmcq-c7-7`.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t7-292` builds a different question from the same sentence — \"**Pain** is the MOST important indicator differentiating:\", keying *Episcleritis vs scleritis*. **Same book line, one bank reading it as a differentiator and the other as a progression marker.** The line supports the second reading literally; the first is an extension of it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Pain is the book's stated most important indicator of scleritis progression, while severity is what visual loss is related to — two different superlatives on the same page, and neither duration nor complications is printed as an indicator.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.54 (Ch.7 Q24)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-sclera',
    stem:"Which of the following conditions is described as being a localized protrusion of the sclera lined by uveal tissue that is blue in color?",
    options:["Scleral nodule","Scleral papule","Staphyloma","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THE STEM CARRIES THREE MISPRINTS IN ONE LINE**, all confirmed at 400 dpi and repaired above as transcription hygiene: the bank prints \"Which of the following **condition**\" (singular after *of the following*), \"described as **bein** a\" for *being*, and a **double space** inside \"(localized  protrusion\". The parenthesis also opens after the article, so \"a\" sits outside the quoted definition. None of it touches the meaning.\n\n**The stem is a near-verbatim lift of the definition, so this is recognition rather than reasoning.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.97, and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` slide 29 in almost identical words:\n\n> A **localized protrusion of the sclera lined by uveal tissue** (i.e.: **bluish** in color) is called **staphyloma.**\n\n**The colour is the diagnostic clue and it has the same cause as blue sclera.** A staphyloma is blue because the uveal tissue lining the protrusion shows through a stretched, thinned scleral wall — the same mechanism p.97 gives for blue sclera generally, applied locally instead of globally. **Blue sclera is a thin sclera; a staphyloma is a thin sclera that has also bulged.**\n\n| Option | Status |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Scleral nodule** | ⚠️ Not a term this course uses. The nodules it names are **episcleral** (in nodular episcleritis, mobile and tender, p.98) and the **nodular** subtype of anterior scleritis. Neither protrudes as a blue bulge |\n| **B. Scleral papule** | ⚠️ Appears nowhere in the module — pure invention. *Papule* is a dermatological word and the chapter never uses it |\n| **C. Staphyloma** | **The key**, defined verbatim |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n⚠️ **A AND B ARE THE ONLY TWO FABRICATED DISTRACTORS IN THE CHAPTER**, which makes this the easiest question in it — the answer is the only real entity on the menu. Measured: `scleral nodule` and `scleral papule` return zero hits across the sclera book chapter and all of `L13,14`.\n\n⚠️ **WHAT THE COURSE DOES NOT TEACH ABOUT STAPHYLOMA, RECORDED SO IT IS NOT INVENTED.** `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` states it plainly: staphyloma is \"**Defined in one line (p.97) with one figure; no anterior / intercalary / ciliary / equatorial / posterior types, no causes**\", and `L13,14`'s own not-covered register at line 721 records that **the TYPES of staphyloma** are absent from the deck as well. So the definition is taught twice and the classification is taught nowhere in either scleral source.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **BUT THE CLASSIFICATION IS EXAMINED ELSEWHERE IN THE MODULE, AND THAT IS THE REASON TO FLAG IT.** Two live questions use type names this chapter never defines: `opqb-t2-112` asks which staphyloma is commonest in **myopia** (key: **posterior**) over a menu of equatorial / posterior / ciliary / intercalary, and `opqb-t7-289` asks the commonest **cause** of posterior staphyloma (key: **high myopia**). **A student who learns staphyloma only from this chapter can answer this question and neither of those.** The gap is real and it is filled by the refraction material, not by chapter 7.\n\n**The types, since the module assumes them without printing them:** staphylomata are named for where the bulge sits — **anterior** (at the limbus), **intercalary** (between limbus and ciliary body), **ciliary** (over the ciliary body), **equatorial** (at the equator, over the vortex-vein exits) and **posterior** (at the posterior pole, the myopic one). *(**Not taken from the course material** — neither scleral source prints any type.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A staphyloma is a localized protrusion of thinned sclera lined by uveal tissue, and it is blue for the same reason a thin sclera is — the uvea shows through. The course defines it in one line and never classifies it, though other questions in the module assume the types.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.54 (Ch.7 Q25; the stem is printed "Which of the following condition is described as bein a (localized  protrusion of the sclera lined by uveal tissue and is blue in color)?", with the double space as shown)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c7-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Where are the Palisades of Vogt located in the eye?",
    options:["Central cornea","Sclera","Limbus","Macula"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS PRINTED UNDER \"7. The sclera\" AND TESTS NOTHING SCLERAL.** It closes the chapter and belongs to conjunctival anatomy, which is why it is filed to `op-conj` rather than `op-sclera` — the project rule is to file by what the question **tests**, not by the heading it sits under or the organ it names. *(Secondary chapter: `op-sclera` — it is printed there and option B is the sclera.)*\n\n⚠️ **ITS ANSWER IS NOT IN THIS CHAPTER'S SOURCES AT ALL.** `palisad` returns **zero hits** in `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch07-sclera.txt` and **zero** in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt`. It **is** taught — in a different book chapter. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.83, conjunctival anatomy:\n\n> The conjunctiva at the **limbus** hosts the **palisades of Vogt** which contain the **stem cells** that provide regenerative cells to the corneal epithelium. Damage to these structures results in **corneal stem cell deficiency** with corneal **superficial vascularization** and **conjunctivalization** of the corneal epithelium.\n\nThat single sentence answers this question and explains why the answer matters.\n\n**The limbus is a border with a job.** It is the corneoscleral junction — the one place p.83 says the bulbar conjunctiva is \"**firmly attached**\" rather than loose — and the palisades are radial ridges of that tissue holding the limbal stem-cell population. Corneal epithelium has no stem cells of its own; it is resurfaced continuously from the limbus inwards. **Destroy the ring and the cornea cannot repair itself**: conjunctival epithelium creeps across it instead, bringing blood vessels with it, and the cornea goes opaque. That is the chain the book compresses into one sentence.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Central cornea** | The tissue the palisades **serve**, not where they sit. Central corneal epithelium is the endpoint of centripetal migration from the limbus |\n| **B. Sclera** | ⚠️ The chapter's heading, and the trap for a reader answering by context rather than by anatomy. The palisades are conjunctival, at the corneoscleral junction |\n| **C. Limbus** | **The key** |\n| **D. Macula** | Retina. Nothing to do with the ocular surface |\n\n⚠️⚠️ **AN EPONYM COLLISION WORTH KNOWING, BECAUSE IT HAS ALREADY MISLED A SEARCH IN THIS PROJECT.** Measured across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters, `palisad|vogt` returns four distinct hits, and **only one of them is this structure**:\n\n| Hit | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| `ophthalmology.pdf` p.83 | **Palisades of Vogt** — the limbal stem-cell niche. This question's answer |\n| `L15,16) Cornea.txt` sl. — \"**Vogt's striae**\" | ⚠️ Vertical **stromal stress lines in keratoconus**. Same eponym, different structure, different chapter, different disease |\n| `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt`, `book\\ch10-uveal-tract.txt` | ⚠️ **Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome** — a uveitis. Same eponym again, and `L13,14` records VKH as *entirely absent* from its own teaching |\n\n**Three unrelated things named Vogt inside one module.** Searching for \"Vogt\" and taking the first hit gets keratoconus or a uveitis.\n\n⚠️ **THE CLOSEST NEIGHBOUR IN THE LIVE CORPUS IS `opmcq-c6-17` — SAME BANK, PREVIOUS CHAPTER.** It asks \"Damage to which of the following structures causes corneal superficial vascularization and conjunctivalization of corneal epithelium?\" and keys **Limbus** from a menu of Limbus / Sclera / Cornea / Conjunctiva. **The two questions key the same word from the same book sentence, one asking where the palisades are and the other asking what damaging them does.** Different stems, different menus, and no shared misprint — flagged for the hub, not folded here.\n\n⚠️ **What the module does with limbal stem cells clinically is taught in the TRAUMA decks, not here.** `L25) Trauma I.txt` gives \"Occlusion of the limbal vessels (**LIMBAL ISCHAEMIA**) and loss of **LIMBAL STEM** cells\" as a consequence of chemical burns and lists **limbal stem cell transplantation** and keratoprosthesis among the treatments; `L26) Trauma II.txt` repeats the transplantation. **The anatomy is in chapter 6, the failure state is in chapter 6, the operation is in the trauma lectures, and the question is printed in chapter 7.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The palisades of Vogt sit at the limbus and hold the stem cells that resurface the corneal epithelium; destroying them causes corneal stem cell deficiency with superficial vascularization and conjunctivalization of the cornea.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.54 (Ch.7 Q26; printed under the chapter heading "7. The sclera" although it tests conjunctival/limbal anatomy)' }

